tomandcatherine wrote on Mon, 15 Sep 2003 19:30:26 GMT in article 
<mro9b.463683$Ho3.75155@sccrnsc03>:
> 
> "Rast" <rast2@hotmail.com> wrote
 
> > Note that *DSM can be obtained by reverse genocide or even from a random
> > dragon, with luck.

> Well... I don't think the point of this spoiler is to completely spoil you
> on the whole game

No, but it's about making effective wishes.  Say you've just beaten the 
castle, and you need SDSM.  You can wish for it, or you can wish for 
genocide or a marker and create SDSM.  The latter is a lot better, and 
should be mentioned.

> > Late-game, spellbook of polymorph is a high-priority wish for a
> spellcaster.
> 
> Wizards are really the only class that can easily and reliably cast
> polymorph

Most any class can cast polymorph (it doesn't have to be 100% reliable) with 
maxed stats and caster gear.  I've ascended the following characters who 
made extensive use of the polymorph spell: R, K, M x3, H, P x4, S.

> -- and they can write it with a magic marker fairly easily by
> the time they're high enough level to cast it.  As per your later
> suggestion (if it can be done in less than 100 marker charges...) this is
> an awful wish.

> Also, a late-game spellcaster can spend all the time in
> the world hanging out at altars fishing for spellbooks.

I think we need to put a value on time, both RL and in-game, otherwise we 
end up lining up 5 krakens in a corridor and repeatedly casting finger of 
death & turn undead at them (the krakens drop items every time; google for 
last year's dev/null high score).

> > I think 2 blessed potions of gain ability is a horrible wish since you
> can
> > get them from alchemy fairly easily.
> 
> Potions of full healing and gain energy / level aren't exactly easy to
> get.

Full healing is (relatively) easy to get from alchemy.  Gain energy/level 
are somewhat rare, but you only need one to turn a stack of !FH into !GA.  

> It certainly isn't a high priority wish, but it's not a "horrible"
> one if your stats (especially int) need improvement right away.

I'm not sure how a stat boost right could ever improve your chances of 
survival more than about 100 other things you could wish for that are way 
better.

I'll bet that "2 blessed potions of gain ability" line was added back when 
alchemy was random and you couldn't effectively create desired potions.  It 
certainly doesn't belong in the spoiler now.

> > Magicbane is worth enchanting over +2, since it boosts damage.
> 
> Magicbane is worthwhile at +2 and +7 only,

It's main value is in magic resistance and curse protection.  The special 
abilities are random enough to not be reliable (kinda like Vorpy), so you 
should seriously considering enchanting it when possible.  I'd WISH for it 
at +2 (rather than +3), but it's completely wrong to say a +4 or +6 
Magicbane is not worthwhile.

> > Mitre of Holiness  --  Awful.
> 
> Better than a generic helm of brilliance.

A generic helm of brilliance doesn't make it harder to wish/sac for 
additional artifacts.

> And don't forget the half damage from pretty much all of Gehennom!

Only priests get that.  And the #invoke ability is pathetic (it needs to 
fill mana up to max and/or increase max, or something a LOT stronger than 
what it does now).  If you're going to wish for an artifact to help you cast 
spells, get the "Eye.

> > Eyes of the Overworld  --  MR, other abilities.  The other neutral
> artifacts
> > are better.
> 
> Astral vision is nothing to be sneezed at.  Neither is free enlightenment.

None of these potential wishes exist in a vacuum.  Since the wish supply is 
typically quite limited, you need to compare each wish with the other things 
you could wish for instead.  In the case of the Eyes, the obvious comparison 
is to the other neutral quest artifacts, plus stuff like *DSM and speed 
boots.

It doesn't ever measure up.

> > Master Key of Thievery  --  TC, half physical damage.  Medium.
> 
> Same or slightly higher than ring of TC.

A lot higher, assuming you're chaotic.  There are basically no other chaotic 
artifacts worth wishing for (some people might say Stormbringer), so it 
doesn't inhibit your ability to wish for other things.  Half physical damage 
is quite good, especially early.

> > Remember, the default value of a wish is one magic marker, which when
> > recharged is worth on average a little over 100 charges.  If you can
> obtain
> > something for less than that, you shouldn't wish for it.  Example:  It's
> far
> > better to reverse-genocide tengu (22 marker charges, 1/5 of a wish) than
> to
> > use a whole wish for a ring of of TC.

> Except that it's likely to take you more like 4 scrolls, since not all
> tengu leave corpses, and not all corpses give you TC.  It also requires
> either some unholy water, or cursed blank paper.  So it's not as easy a
> choice as you make it out to be.

You get 4-6 tengu per scroll, and half will leave corpses.  I'm not sure 
what the chance of getting TC from a tengu corpse is; I think it depends on 
the level of the tengu.  But I seriously doubt the expected number of tengu 
corpses needed is 10 or more. 

> Depending on the stage of the game, you may or may not have the ability to
> write scrolls of charging, and you may or may not have a large amount of
> paper to be writing scrolls, and you may not even know what those scrolls
> are.

If we're still in the early/mid game, you don't want to be wishing for TC 
because there are many more urgent things you can wish for.


